export const KNOWLEDGE = "<notes>\n<note file=\"agent-skills.md\" title=\"Agent skills\">\nAn agent skill packages procedural knowledge (how a specific job is done, in what order, with what judgment) as a SKILL.md file in a folder. LLMs already know many facts; what they lack is the organisation-specific \"how\", such as a 47-step workflow for a compliant financial report.\nSources:\n- What AI Agent Skills Are and How They Work, [00:00] to [01:06] (procedural knowledge gap), [01:06] to [03:12] (SKILL.md, front matter, description as trigger), [03:12] to [04:12] (instructions, optional folders), [04:12] to [06:18] (progressive disclosure), [06:18] to [08:24] (MCP, RAG, fine-tuning, open standard), [08:24] to [10:29] (memory analogy), [10:29] (security)\n</note>\n<note file=\"ai-agents.md\" title=\"AI agents\">\nAn AI agent is a compound AI system in which an LLM is put in charge of the control logic: it reasons about a problem, plans, acts through tools, observes the results and adjusts, instead of following a path fixed by a programmer.\nSources:\n- What are AI Agents?, [00:00] to [02:07] (monolithic versus compound, vacation example), [02:07] to [04:11] (modularity, RAG as compound system), [04:11] to [06:13] (control logic, LLM in charge, think fast or slow), [06:13] to [08:18] (reason, act, memory, ReAct), [09:20] to [11:24] (sunscreen example, autonomy trade-off, human in the loop)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [191:32] to [192:33] (agents doing longer tasks, humans as supervisors)\n</note>\n<note file=\"ai-ml-genai.md\" title=\"AI, machine learning and generative AI\">\nThese are nested fields. Artificial intelligence (AI) is the broad effort to make computers match or exceed human abilities such as learning, inferring and reasoning. Machine learning (ML) is a subset where the machine learns patterns from data instead of being explicitly programmed. Deep learning is a subset of ML that uses neural networks with many layers. Generative AI, built on foundation models, is the newest layer: it generates new content.\nSources:\n- AI, Machine Learning, Deep Learning and Generative AI Explained, [01:02] (AI definition), [02:03] to [04:06] (ML, outliers, cyber security), [04:06] to [05:06] (deep learning), [06:08] to [08:10] (foundation models, generative AI, deepfakes), [09:13] (adoption curve)\n- Anthropic Academy, What We Mean by AI lesson\n</note>\n<note file=\"ai-stack.md\" title=\"Complete AI systems (the AI stack)\">\nThe AI stack is the set of layers needed to build an AI system that solves real problems rather than just generating answers: infrastructure, models, data, orchestration and application. Choices at every layer affect the solution's quality, speed, cost and safety.\nSources:\n- What Is an AI Stack?, [00:00] to [02:06] (the layers with the drug-discovery example), [02:06] to [03:12] (quality, speed, cost, safety; infrastructure options), [03:12] to [04:15] (model choices), [04:15] to [05:18] (data layer), [05:18] to [06:20] (orchestration, MCP), [06:20] to [08:25] (application layer)\n- Anthropic Academy, Knowledge lesson (knowledge cutoff)\n</note>\n<note file=\"benchmarks.md\" title=\"Benchmarks\">\nLLM benchmarks are standardised frameworks for assessing LLM performance: they supply a task, evaluate the model's output with a specific metric and produce a score (often 0 to 100). They let you compare models for a task and guide fine-tuning.\nSources:\n- What are Large Language Model Benchmarks?, [00:00] (definition, purpose), [00:00] to [02:06] (three steps, zero-shot, few-shot, metrics), [02:06] to [05:09] (track team and science test examples), [05:09] (limitations)\n- What Are Large Reasoning Models?, [07:27] (reasoning models top benchmarks)\n- LLM as a Judge: Scaling AI Evaluation Strategies (see evaluating-ai-outputs.md)\n</note>\n<note file=\"business-workflows.md\" title=\"Business workflows (AI agents for real-world problems)\">\nIn production, successful AI agents are not standalone decision makers but coordination layers: they maintain context, orchestrate actions across systems, enforce rules and decide when control passes to a human.\nSources:\n- Building AI Agents for Real-World Problems & Workflows, [00:00] to [01:06] (demo versus production, core challenges), [01:06] to [02:11] (coordination layer, onboarding), [02:11] to [04:14] (policy-governed execution, IT support), [04:14] to [06:26] (invoice processing, triage), [06:26] to [07:29] (characteristics)\n- AI Agents vs Business Rules, [06:25] to [09:39] (hybrid flow with guardrails and human in the loop)\n</note>\n<note file=\"capabilities-and-limitations.md\" title=\"AI capabilities and limitations (Anthropic Academy course)\">\nThe Anthropic Academy course frames generative AI around four properties: Next Token Prediction, Knowledge, Working Memory and Steerability. Each sits on a continuum from capability to limitation, and the same mechanism produces both the strength and the weakness. AI is neither uniformly capable nor uniformly unreliable; calibrated trust means locating your task on each continuum. The course is the machine-side companion to the AI Fluency 4D framework (Delegation, Description, Discernment, Diligence).\nSources:\n- Anthropic Academy, AI Capabilities and Limitations: What We Mean by AI (lesson 2), How AI Gets Its Character (lesson 3), Next Token Prediction (lesson 4), Knowledge (lesson 6), Working Memory (lesson 8), Steerability (lesson 10), When Properties Collide (lesson 12). Files in knowledge/raw/anthropic-academy/.\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [98:50] (vague recollection versus working memory), [124:20] to [125:22] (jagged edges: 9.11 versus 9.9)\n</note>\n<note file=\"context-windows.md\" title=\"Context windows\">\nThe context window is an LLM's working memory: the maximum number of tokens the model can pay attention to at one time. It sets how long a conversation can run before earlier details are lost.\nSources:\n- What is a Context Window?, [00:00] to [02:09] (working memory, forgetting, hallucination), [03:12] to [05:23] (tokens, tokenizer, 1.5 rule), [07:33] to [08:36] (what fills the window), [08:36] to [10:46] (quadratic compute, lost in the middle, safety)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [98:50] to [100:51] (vague recollection versus working memory), [193:34] (no learning at test time)\n- Anthropic Academy, Working Memory lesson\n</note>\n<note file=\"embeddings.md\" title=\"Embeddings\">\nA word embedding represents a word as a numeric vector so that semantic relationships are preserved: words with similar meanings sit close together, and the distance and direction between vectors encode how similar they are.\nSources:\n- What are Word Embeddings?, [00:00] (definition, why numbers), [01:02] to [02:04] (applications), [02:04] to [03:05] (training, toy example), [04:06] to [05:09] (TF-IDF), [05:09] to [06:10] (prediction-based, word2vec, GloVe), [07:13] (contextual embeddings)\n- What is a Vector Database?, [01:01] to [02:03] (vectors and embeddings)\n</note>\n<note file=\"evaluating-ai-outputs.md\" title=\"Evaluating AI outputs (LLM as a judge)\">\nLLM as a judge means using an LLM to evaluate the outputs of (other) LLMs, such as chatbot replies or summaries. It addresses two problems: traditional metrics may not fit the task, and manual labelling of hundreds of outputs is slow.\nSources:\n- LLM as a Judge: Scaling AI Evaluation Strategies, [00:00] to [01:01] (why, two strategies), [01:01] to [02:02] (direct assessment, pairwise, EvalAssist research), [02:02] to [03:04] (scale, flexibility), [03:04] to [05:06] (nuance, BLEU and ROUGE, biases), [05:06] (positional swaps, vigilance)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [177:17] to [180:20] (reward model as simulator of human preferences, lossy simulation)\n- What are Large Language Model Benchmarks?, [05:09] (limits of fixed benchmarks)\n</note>\n<note file=\"explainability.md\" title=\"Explainability (explainable AI, XAI)\">\nExplainable AI (XAI) is the set of methods that let humans understand how an AI model reaches its results, so they can trust (or challenge) them. Many models are black boxes: not even the engineers who built them can fully explain a specific result.\nSources:\n- What is Explainable AI?, [00:00] to [02:05] (black box, fraud example, XAI), [02:05] to [04:09] (prediction accuracy, LIME, traceability, DeepLIFT, decision understanding), [04:09] to [05:16] (dashboard, counterfactual, drift), [05:16] to [06:17] (use cases, regulation, right to challenge)\n- AI Agents vs Business Rules, [04:18] (the rule is also the explanation)\n- AI, Machine Learning, Deep Learning and Generative AI Explained, [05:06] (deep learning hard to decompose)\n</note>\n<note file=\"hallucinations.md\" title=\"Hallucinations\">\nA hallucination is an LLM output that deviates from facts or contextual logic: plausible-sounding but wrong or invented content, from minor inconsistencies to completely fabricated statements.\nSources:\n- Why Large Language Models Hallucinate, [01:02] to [03:11] (definition, types), [03:11] to [05:18] (causes), [06:21] to [08:25] (prompts, temperature, multi-shot)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [84:36] to [87:39] (imitating the answer format, Meta's \"I don't know\" fix), [93:44] (tool use as mitigation)\n- Anthropic Academy, Next Token Prediction and Knowledge lessons\n</note>\n<note file=\"how-llms-work.md\" title=\"How LLMs work\">\nA large language model (LLM) is a foundation model specialised for text and text-like data such as code. It is a neural network (for GPT, a transformer) trained on a very large text corpus to predict the next token. \"Large\" refers both to the data (terabytes to petabytes of text) and to the parameter count (GPT-3: 175 billion parameters, 45 TB of training data).\nSources:\n- How Large Language Models Work, [00:00] to [04:13] (foundation model, data, architecture, training, fine-tuning, business uses)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [01:02] (pre-training data), [08:10] to [14:19] (tokenization), [26:30] to [29:37] (inference, sampling), [49:59] (knowledge in parameters), [146:45] (three stages compared to textbooks: exposition, worked solutions, practice problems)\n- Anthropic Academy, How AI Gets Its Character and Next Token Prediction lessons\n</note>\n<note file=\"mcp-and-apis.md\" title=\"MCP and APIs\">\nAn API (application programming interface) is a set of rules describing how one system requests information or services from another. MCP (Model Context Protocol), an open standard introduced by Anthropic in late 2024, standardises how AI applications connect LLMs to external data and tools. The video's metaphor: MCP is a USB-C port for AI applications.\nSources:\n- MCP vs API: Simplifying AI Agent Integration with External Data, [00:00] to [02:13] (definition, USB-C, host, client, server), [02:13] to [05:21] (context and tools, primitives, discovery), [05:21] to [07:33] (APIs, REST), [07:33] to [10:38] (similarities, purpose-built, discovery), [10:38] to [11:40] (standardisation, wrappers, layers)\n- What AI Agent Skills Are and How They Work, [06:18] (MCP is about what the agent can reach)\n</note>\n<note file=\"memory.md\" title=\"Memory (the four types of agent memory)\">\nAgent memory is how an AI agent keeps and uses information beyond a single model call. The CoALA framework (Cognitive Architectures for Language Agents, Princeton) names four types, mirroring human memory: working, semantic, procedural and episodic.\nSources:\n- The Four Types of Memory Every AI Agent Needs, [00:00] to [02:09] (human analogy, CoALA), [02:09] to [03:13] (working memory, RAM), [03:13] to [04:17] (semantic memory, CLAUDE.md), [04:17] to [06:20] (procedural memory, skills), [06:20] to [08:32] (episodic memory, forgetting), [08:32] to [09:35] (which agents need which)\n- Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering, [04:06] (short-term and long-term memory)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [193:34] (no learning at test time)\n</note>\n<note file=\"probabilistic-vs-deterministic.md\" title=\"Probabilistic versus deterministic outcomes (AI agents versus business rules)\">\nA deterministic system always gives the same output for the same input: the output is a fixed function of the input. A business rules engine is deterministic. An AI agent built on an LLM is probabilistic: it samples from a probability distribution over possible responses, so the same request run twice can give different answers.\nSources:\n- AI Agents vs Business Rules: Which Should Make Decisions?, [00:00] to [02:08] (rules versus agents), [02:08] to [03:11] (deterministic, probabilistic), [04:18] to [05:21] (when to use each, audits, testing, cost), [06:25] to [09:39] (hybrid flow, guardrails, human in the loop)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [48:58] (the system is stochastic: same prefix, different outputs)\n</note>\n<note file=\"prompt-injection.md\" title=\"Prompt injection\">\nPrompt injection is an attack in which someone feeds an LLM instructions that override or bend its intended behaviour. OWASP (the Open Worldwide Application Security Project) ranks it as the number one vulnerability for LLM applications. It is like social engineering, but aimed at a computer.\nSources:\n- What Is a Prompt Injection Attack?, [00:00] to [01:02] (SUV example), [01:02] to [02:02] (OWASP top 10), [02:02] to [04:05] (social engineering, jailbreaks, instructions versus input), [04:05] to [06:08] (direct and indirect, consequences), [06:08] to [10:14] (defences)\n- What is a Context Window?, [09:43] to [10:46] (long context and jailbreaking)\n- What AI Agent Skills Are and How They Work, [10:29] (prompt injection in public skills)\n</note>\n<note file=\"prompt-vs-context-engineering.md\" title=\"Prompt engineering versus context engineering\">\nPrompt engineering is the craft of wording the input text itself: instructions, examples and formatting cues that steer the model. Context engineering is the broader, system-level discipline of programmatically assembling everything the LLM sees at inference time: prompts, but also retrieved documents, memory, state and tools.\nSources:\n- Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering, [00:00] to [02:00] (definitions, Agent Graeme), [02:00] to [04:06] (role, few-shot, chain of thought, constraints), [04:06] to [06:06] (memory, state, RAG), [06:06] to [07:09] (tools, dynamic prompts, 80/20)\n- Anthropic Academy, Steerability lesson\n</note>\n<note file=\"rag.md\" title=\"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)\">\nRAG is a framework that makes LLM answers more accurate and up to date by first retrieving relevant content from a content store and then generating the answer from that content together with the user's question.\nSources:\n- What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation?, [00:00] to [02:03] (no source, out of date), [02:03] to [04:08] (content store, retrieve then generate, three-part prompt), [04:08] to [05:10] (updating data, sources), [05:10] to [06:12] (\"I don't know\", retriever quality)\n- Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering, [05:06] (hybrid search, relevant sections only)\n- Anthropic Academy, Knowledge lesson (retrieval and tools patch knowledge gaps)\n</note>\n<note file=\"reasoning-models.md\" title=\"Reasoning models (large reasoning models, LRMs)\">\nA large reasoning model (LRM) is an LLM trained to \"think before it talks\": before producing the final answer it generates an internal chain of thought in which it plans, weighs options, tests hypotheses and checks calculations. It still predicts tokens, but it spends extra tokens on reasoning first.\nSources:\n- What Are Large Reasoning Models?, [00:00] to [01:06] (LLM versus LRM), [01:06] to [02:13] (when it matters, cost), [02:13] to [04:14] (training: tuning, RLHF, process reward models), [04:14] to [05:19] (distillation), [05:19] to [07:27] (test-time compute, pros and cons)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [112:07] (spreading computation over tokens), [149:47] to [150:49] (accuracy and longer responses in RL), [159:57] (when to use thinking models)\n- Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering, [03:04] (chain-of-thought prompting before reasoning models)\n</note>\n<note file=\"selecting-business-applications.md\" title=\"Selecting business applications (the right AI use case)\">\nSelecting a business application means matching the business problem to the right tool, technique or solution. Generative AI is not always the right choice; the goal is better outcomes and the best return on investment (ROI).\nSources:\n- Select the right AI use case for your business, [00:00] (playmaker metaphor, ROI, GenAI not always right), [01:05] (financial forecast and optimisation examples, tech to problem), [02:08] (TCO, needs, capabilities, integration, skills), [03:11] (adjust to reality)\n- AI Agents vs Business Rules, [04:18] (when rules are better)\n- What are AI Agents?, [10:22] to [11:24] (narrow problems favour programmatic control)\n</note>\n<note file=\"synthetic-data.md\" title=\"Synthetic data\">\nSynthetic data is information that is artificially generated (by computer, from existing data sets or from algorithms and models) to replicate the properties and characteristics of real-world data, rather than being produced by real events.\nSources:\n- What is Synthetic Data?, [00:03] to [01:06] (definition, Southampton example), [01:06] to [02:07] (derived from data or algorithms, why), [02:07] to [04:10] (advantages, uses, Gartner, bias), [04:10] (challenges, Leicester City), [04:10] to [06:12] (generation: noise, GANs, statistical generators)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [128:24] (LLMs help create training conversations)\n- What Are Large Reasoning Models?, [04:14] (distillation)\n</note>\n<note file=\"tool-calling.md\" title=\"Tool calling\">\nTool calling lets an LLM use real-time data and actions (databases, APIs, code) that it does not have in its parameters. The model does not run the tool itself: it recommends which tool to call and with what inputs; the application (or a library) executes it and feeds the result back.\nSources:\n- What is Tool Calling? Connecting LLMs to Your Data, [00:00] (flow), [01:03] to [02:08] (tool definition, weather example), [02:08] to [04:18] (hallucinated calls, embedded tool calling)\n- Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (Karpathy), [93:44] to [95:48] (special tokens for search), [118:13] (prefer tools over mental arithmetic, counting)\n- AI Agents vs Business Rules, [08:33] (agent decides which tools, in which order)\n</note>\n<note file=\"transformers.md\" title=\"Transformers\">\nA transformer is a deep learning architecture that transforms one sequence into another (for example an English sentence into French). It is the \"T\" in GPT (generative pre-trained transformer). Its defining feature is the attention mechanism.\nSources:\n- What are Transformers?, [00:01] (GPT, autoregressive), [01:03] to [03:03] (encoder, decoder, sequence-to-sequence), [03:03] (semi-supervised, RNN comparison), [04:04] (attention, parallel processing, applications)\n- How Large Language Models Work, [02:04] to [03:07] (each word considered in relation to every other word)\n- What is a Context Window?, [05:23] to [06:23] (self-attention weights)\n</note>\n<note file=\"vector-databases.md\" title=\"Vector databases\">\nA vector database stores data as vectors (numeric arrays, created as embeddings) so it can search by similarity of meaning rather than by exact matches. It is the newest step after SQL (structured tables), NoSQL (unstructured documents) and graph databases (nodes and relationships).\nSources:\n- What is a Vector Database?, [00:00] to [01:01] (SQL, NoSQL, graph, vector), [01:01] to [03:04] (vectors and embeddings), [03:04] to [04:07] (use cases), [05:08] to [07:10] (flexibility, scalability, speed, polyglot)\n- Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering, [04:06] (long-term memory in vector databases)\n- What are Word Embeddings?, [02:04] (similar documents, recommendations)\n</note>\n</notes>\n\n<question_bank>\nQ01 [how-llms-work | L1] In plain terms, what is a large language model and how does it produce text?\nAnswer: An LLM is a foundation model for text and code: a neural network (for GPT, a transformer) trained on huge amounts of text to predict the next token. It generates an answer one token at a time, each new token conditioned on everything before it.\nSource: How Large Language Models Work [00:00], [02:04] to [03:07]\n\nQ02 [how-llms-work | L1] What is a parameter, and what does the \"large\" in large language model refer to?\nAnswer: A parameter is a value the model adjusts as it learns. \"Large\" refers to the size of the training data (terabytes or more of text) and the number of parameters; GPT-3 has 175 billion parameters and was trained on about 45 TB of data.\nSource: How Large Language Models Work [01:01] to [02:04]\n\nQ03 [how-llms-work | L2] What is the difference between pre-training and fine-tuning, and why does a company need both to get a useful assistant?\nAnswer: Pre-training teaches a model general language and knowledge by predicting the next token across vast text; the result is a document completer (Karpathy's \"internet document simulator\") with no idea of helping you. Fine-tuning on a smaller, specific dataset (for an assistant, example conversations written to labelling instructions) turns it into a specialist or helpful assistant.\nSource: How Large Language Models Work [03:07] to [04:13]; Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [60:12] to [63:16], [146:45]; Anthropic Academy, How AI Gets Its Character\n\nQ04 [how-llms-work | L3] Karpathy describes three training stages using a textbook analogy. What are they, and what does each contribute?\nAnswer: Pre-training is like reading all the exposition in textbooks: it builds a knowledge base. Supervised fine-tuning is like studying worked solutions from experts: the model learns to imitate expert answers. Reinforcement learning is like doing the practice problems: the model tries, gets feedback on what works and discovers its own ways of solving problems.\nSource: Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [146:45]\n\nQ05 [ai-ml-genai | L1] How do AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI relate to each other?\nAnswer: They are nested. AI is the broad field of making computers match or exceed human intelligence. Machine learning is a subset where the machine learns patterns from data rather than being programmed. Deep learning is a subset of ML using multi-layer neural networks. Generative AI, built on foundation models, is the most recent layer and produces new content.\nSource: AI, Machine Learning, Deep Learning and Generative AI Explained [01:02] to [06:08]\n\nQ06 [ai-ml-genai | L2] Give one task where classic machine learning is a better fit than generative AI, and explain why.\nAnswer: Spotting outliers or making predictions from patterns, for example flagging unusual user behaviour in cyber security or an odd transaction. ML is built to find patterns and anomalies; it does not need to generate content, so it is the natural fit.\nSource: AI, Machine Learning, Deep Learning and Generative AI Explained [03:05] to [04:06]\n\nQ07 [ai-ml-genai | L3] Some say generative AI only regurgitates its training data. How would you respond?\nAnswer: The video's analogy is music: every note already exists, yet new songs are composed by recombining them. Generative models similarly recombine learned patterns into content that is new. Karpathy adds that a base model can produce text that was not verbatim in any training document. The caveat is that new content is not necessarily true.\nSource: AI, Machine Learning, Deep Learning and Generative AI Explained [07:08]; Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [28:35]\n\nQ08 [transformers | L1] What is a transformer, and what is its key mechanism?\nAnswer: A transformer is a deep learning architecture that transforms one sequence into another, for example translating a sentence. Its key mechanism is attention, which works out how each token relates to the others in the sequence to give it context.\nSource: What are Transformers? [01:03], [04:04]\n\nQ09 [transformers | L2] Why were transformers a big improvement over recurrent neural networks for training on large datasets?\nAnswer: RNNs must process input in order, one step at a time. Transformers use attention and do not need to process the sequence in order, so they can process sequences in parallel, which vastly speeds up training.\nSource: What are Transformers? [03:03] to [04:04]\n\nQ10 [transformers | L3] A colleague says translation is just a lookup from one language to another. Why does that fail, and how does an encoder-decoder transformer handle it?\nAnswer: Word order and phrasing differ between languages, so word-for-word lookup breaks. In an encoder-decoder transformer the encoder layers build encodings of which parts of the input relate to each other, and the decoder uses that context to generate the output sequence token by token.\nSource: What are Transformers? [02:03] to [03:03]\n\nQ11 [context-windows | L1] What is a context window, and in what unit is it measured?\nAnswer: It is the model's working memory: the maximum amount of input it can pay attention to at once, including the conversation so far. It is measured in tokens, which can be characters, parts of words, whole words or short phrases. Roughly 1.5 tokens per English word.\nSource: What is a Context Window? [00:00], [03:12] to [05:23]\n\nQ12 [context-windows | L2] Besides your own messages, what else takes up space in a context window?\nAnswer: The model's own responses, the system prompt (often hidden), attached documents or source code, and supplementary information retrieved for RAG. A few long documents can fill a window quickly.\nSource: What is a Context Window? [07:33] to [08:36]\n\nQ13 [context-windows | L3] If bigger context windows let you include more, why not always use the largest one?\nAnswer: Three challenges. Compute grows quadratically with sequence length (double the tokens, about four times the processing). Performance can drop: models use information at the start and end best and degrade on content in the middle. Safety: a longer context is a larger attack surface where harmful instructions can be buried.\nSource: What is a Context Window? [08:36] to [10:46]\n\nQ14 [context-windows | L3] Karpathy contrasts knowledge in the parameters with knowledge in the context window. What is the practical lesson for a user?\nAnswer: Knowledge in the parameters is a vague recollection, like something read months ago; content in the context window is working memory the model can access directly. So if you want an accurate summary or answer about a specific document, paste the document in rather than relying on the model's memory.\nSource: Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [98:50] to [100:51]\n\nQ15 [hallucinations | L1] What is an LLM hallucination? Give an example.\nAnswer: An output that deviates from facts or contextual logic: plausible-sounding but wrong or invented content. Example: saying the Moon is 54 million km from Earth, which is actually the distance to Mars.\nSource: Why Large Language Models Hallucinate [00:00] to [01:02]\n\nQ16 [hallucinations | L2] Name three causes of hallucinations.\nAnswer: Data quality (training data with noise, errors, bias or gaps, and no way to verify); the generation method (decoding such as beam search or sampling trades accuracy against fluency or novelty); and input context (unclear, inconsistent or contradictory prompts).\nSource: Why Large Language Models Hallucinate [03:11] to [05:18]\n\nQ17 [hallucinations | L2] As a user, what can you do to reduce hallucinations?\nAnswer: Give clear, specific prompts with the necessary context; use a lower temperature for more conservative, focused output; use multi-shot prompting with several examples of the desired output; and ground the model in sources, for example with RAG or tools.\nSource: Why Large Language Models Hallucinate [06:21] to [08:25]; What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation? [05:10]\n\nQ18 [hallucinations | L3] Why does a model state a made-up fact confidently instead of saying \"I don't know\", and how did Meta reduce this for Llama 3?\nAnswer: In fine-tuning the model imitates training conversations in which the assistant always answers confidently, so it imitates the format of an answer even when it does not know. The network may internally \"sense\" uncertainty, but that is not wired to saying so. Meta probed which facts the model did not know and added training examples where the correct answer is \"I don't know\".\nSource: Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [84:36] to [87:39]\n\nQ19 [probabilistic-vs-deterministic | L1] What is the difference between a deterministic and a probabilistic system? Which one is an LLM-based agent?\nAnswer: A deterministic system always gives the same output for the same input; the output is a fixed function of the input, like a business rules engine. A probabilistic system picks from a probability distribution over possible outputs, so the same request can give different answers. An LLM-based agent is probabilistic.\nSource: AI Agents vs Business Rules [02:08] to [03:11]\n\nQ20 [probabilistic-vs-deterministic | L2] Why might a regulated company prefer a business rules engine for loan eligibility checks?\nAnswer: The decision is well defined and the inputs are structured. The rule that fired is itself the explanation, which suits audits. Determinism means each branch can be unit tested and behaves the same in production. And there is no model inference cost.\nSource: AI Agents vs Business Rules [03:11] to [05:21]\n\nQ21 [probabilistic-vs-deterministic | L3] Design a refund process that uses both business rules and an AI agent. Where does each go, and why?\nAnswer: Rules first, because they are cheap and fast: clear-cut cases are approved or declined immediately. Only cases the rules cannot decide (messy or unstructured data such as free text or a photo, unanticipated situations) are escalated to an agent, which can use tools. The agent's recommendation then passes deterministic guardrails, for example anything above a value threshold goes to a human in the loop before the final decision.\nSource: AI Agents vs Business Rules [06:25] to [09:39]\n\nQ22 [embeddings | L1] What is a word embedding, and why do we need it?\nAnswer: A representation of a word as a numeric vector that captures meaning, so similar words sit close together in vector space. It is needed because most machine learning algorithms cannot process raw text; they need numbers.\nSource: What are Word Embeddings? [00:00]\n\nQ23 [embeddings | L2] What is the difference between frequency-based embeddings like TF-IDF and prediction-based embeddings like word2vec?\nAnswer: TF-IDF scores a word by how frequent it is in one document and how rare across the corpus, so \"espresso\" scores high in a coffee document and \"the\" scores low. Prediction-based embeddings learn from context by predicting words: word2vec's CBOW predicts a word from its context and skip-gram predicts the context from a word, capturing semantic relationships such as dog with bark and tail.\nSource: What are Word Embeddings? [04:06] to [06:10]\n\nQ24 [embeddings | L3] Why are contextual embeddings from transformers better than static word2vec embeddings for a word like \"bank\"?\nAnswer: Static embeddings give each word one fixed vector, so \"bank\" has the same representation for a financial bank and a river bank. Contextual embeddings change the representation depending on the surrounding words, capturing the intended meaning, which improves NLP tasks.\nSource: What are Word Embeddings? [07:13]\n\nQ25 [vector-databases | L1] What is a vector database, and how does it differ from a SQL database?\nAnswer: A vector database stores data (text, images, audio) as vectors or embeddings and searches by similarity of meaning. A SQL database stores structured data in tables and matches exact values.\nSource: What is a Vector Database? [00:00] to [02:03]\n\nQ26 [vector-databases | L2] Name three use cases and three benefits of vector databases.\nAnswer: Use cases: chatbots and natural language processing, image and voice recognition, similarity search and recommendation engines. Benefits: flexibility (ingest any unstructured data), scalability (millions or billions of vectors) and speed (indexed, low-latency queries).\nSource: What is a Vector Database? [03:04] to [07:10]\n\nQ27 [vector-databases | L3] Should a company move its accounting ledger into a vector database? Why or why not?\nAnswer: No. Vector databases are for similarity search over unstructured content. Structured transactional data belongs in relational databases. The video recommends a polyglot architecture: use the vector database alongside other databases, for example for policies and documents an assistant retrieves, not as a replacement.\nSource: What is a Vector Database? [07:10]\n\nQ28 [rag | L1] What does retrieval-augmented generation mean?\nAnswer: Before generating, the system retrieves relevant content from a content store (the internet or a closed set of documents) and gives it to the LLM together with the user's question, so the answer is grounded in that content.\nSource: What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation? [02:03] to [04:08]\n\nQ29 [rag | L2] Which two LLM problems does RAG address, and how?\nAnswer: Being out of date: new information is added by updating the data store, without retraining the model. Having no source: the model is instructed to use primary source data and can give evidence for its answer, which makes hallucination and data leakage less likely.\nSource: What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation? [01:00], [04:08] to [05:10]\n\nQ30 [rag | L3] What can go wrong with RAG even when the model itself is good?\nAnswer: If the retriever is not good enough, the model gets poor grounding and gives poor answers, or it says \"I don't know\" to a question that could have been answered. That is why both the retriever and the generator need improving. Retrieved content can also carry indirect prompt injections.\nSource: What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation? [05:10] to [06:12]; What Is a Prompt Injection Attack? [05:08]\n\nQ31 [prompt-vs-context-engineering | L1] What is the difference between prompt engineering and context engineering?\nAnswer: Prompt engineering is crafting the input text: instructions, examples, formatting cues. Context engineering is the system-level discipline of programmatically assembling everything the model sees at inference: prompts plus retrieved documents, memory, state and tools.\nSource: Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering [00:00], [02:00]\n\nQ32 [prompt-vs-context-engineering | L2] Name and briefly explain three prompt engineering techniques.\nAnswer: Role assignment (tell the model who it is, for example a senior security reviewer); few-shot examples (2 or 3 input and output pairs that show the format); chain of thought (\"think step by step\" to avoid jumping to conclusions); constraint setting (\"max 100 words\", \"only use the provided context\"). Any three.\nSource: Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering [02:00] to [04:06]\n\nQ33 [prompt-vs-context-engineering | L3] The travel agent booked a 900 euro hotel that breaks company policy. Was that a prompt engineering or a context engineering failure, and how would you fix it?\nAnswer: Context engineering: the agent never had the travel policy. The fix is to make the policy available, for example a JSON file with maximum hotel rates retrieved via RAG, and to give the agent tools such as a calendar or conference lookup. No wording of the prompt can supply facts the model does not have.\nSource: Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering [01:00] to [02:00]\n\nQ34 [tool-calling | L1] What is tool calling?\nAnswer: A technique that connects an LLM to real-time data and actions (APIs, databases, code). The app sends the messages with a list of tool definitions; the model recommends a tool call; the app executes it and sends the result back; the model then gives a final answer or asks for another tool.\nSource: What is Tool Calling? [00:00] to [01:03]\n\nQ35 [tool-calling | L2] What goes into a tool definition, and why is the description important?\nAnswer: The tool's name, a description of what it does and when to use it, and its input parameters. The description is how the model decides whether and how to use the tool.\nSource: What is Tool Calling? [01:03]; Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering [06:06]\n\nQ36 [tool-calling | L3] What are the risks of traditional tool calling, and how does embedded tool calling reduce them?\nAnswer: The model can hallucinate or make incorrect tool calls. In embedded tool calling a library or framework sits between the application and the LLM, holds the tool definitions, executes the calls itself and retries when needed, which reduces wrong calls.\nSource: What is Tool Calling? [02:08] to [04:18]\n\nQ37 [tool-calling | L3] Why does Karpathy prefer that a model uses a code tool instead of doing arithmetic or counting in its head?\nAnswer: Each token gets only a small, fixed amount of computation in one forward pass, so asking the model to count or calculate in a single token asks too much. A Python interpreter computes exactly; the model only has to write the code.\nSource: Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [112:07], [118:13]\n\nQ38 [mcp-and-apis | L1] What is MCP, and what metaphor is often used for it?\nAnswer: Model Context Protocol, an open standard introduced by Anthropic in late 2024 that standardises how AI applications connect LLMs to external data and tools. It is compared to a USB-C port: one standard connection for many different peripherals.\nSource: MCP vs API [00:00] to [01:03]\n\nQ39 [mcp-and-apis | L2] What are the three primitives an MCP server can expose?\nAnswer: Tools (functions the AI can call, such as get_weather), resources (read-only data such as files or database schemas) and prompt templates (predefined suggested prompts). Many servers only expose tools.\nSource: MCP vs API [03:15] to [05:21]\n\nQ40 [mcp-and-apis | L3] Is MCP a replacement for APIs? Explain the relationship and the main differences.\nAnswer: No, they are layers. Many MCP servers are wrappers around existing APIs (the GitHub MCP server translates tool calls into GitHub REST requests). Both are client-server abstractions. Differences: MCP is purpose-built for LLM apps, supports dynamic discovery at runtime (tools/list) and is standardised across servers, whereas each API is unique and usually has no runtime discovery, so clients must be updated by developers.\nSource: MCP vs API [09:35] to [11:40]\n\nQ41 [ai-agents | L1] What is an AI agent, and what three capabilities does it have?\nAnswer: A system where an LLM is in charge of the control logic: it plans and decides the steps itself. Its capabilities are to reason (plan and think through steps), to act (call tools) and to access memory (reasoning logs and conversation history).\nSource: What are AI Agents? [04:11] to [07:18]\n\nQ42 [ai-agents | L2] What is a compound AI system, and how does an agent differ from a fixed RAG pipeline?\nAnswer: A compound AI system combines a model with other components such as databases, tools and verifiers instead of relying on the model alone. A typical RAG pipeline has programmatic control logic defined by a human (always search the vacation database), so it fails on unexpected queries. An agent lets the LLM decide the path.\nSource: What are AI Agents? [02:07] to [05:11]\n\nQ43 [ai-agents | L3] When would you NOT use an agentic approach?\nAnswer: For narrow, well-defined problems where every query should be handled the same way. A programmatic path is more efficient and predictable there, while an agent may loop and iterate unnecessarily. Agents pay off for complex tasks with many possible paths, such as solving GitHub issues.\nSource: What are AI Agents? [10:22] to [11:24]\n\nQ44 [ai-agents | L2] Describe how a ReAct agent works.\nAnswer: ReAct combines reasoning and acting. The model is prompted to think slowly and plan, then act, possibly calling a tool, then observe the result (which may be wrong or an error), and decide whether it answers the question or whether to adjust the plan, looping until a final answer.\nSource: What are AI Agents? [07:18] to [09:20]\n\nQ45 [business-workflows | L1] Why do many AI agents that work in demos fall short in production?\nAnswer: Not because the technology is incapable, but because real problems span multiple systems, involve policies, approvals and rules, have to fit existing workflows, and need a human in the loop.\nSource: Building AI Agents for Real-World Problems & Workflows [00:00] to [01:06]\n\nQ46 [business-workflows | L2] In invoice processing, where does an AI agent add the most value, and where is the real difficulty?\nAnswer: The happy path (extract data, match against records, validate against rules, route approval, update downstream systems) is straightforward. The real difficulty is exceptions: missing, mismatched or non-standard data. The agent adds value by handling predictable flows consistently and surfacing only the exceptions for human review.\nSource: Building AI Agents for Real-World Problems & Workflows [04:14] to [05:24]\n\nQ47 [business-workflows | L3] The video says \"the hard part isn't reasoning\". What is the hard part, and what characteristics do successful production agents share?\nAnswer: The hard part is reliably orchestrating multiple systems while respecting policy and timing constraints. Successful agents are narrowly scoped, orchestrate across systems, apply rules, keep a human in the loop and are designed for integration rather than isolation; they act as coordination layers rather than standalone decision makers.\nSource: Building AI Agents for Real-World Problems & Workflows [02:11], [06:26]\n\nQ48 [agent-skills | L1] What is an agent skill, and what are the two mandatory fields in a SKILL.md file?\nAnswer: A skill packages procedural knowledge, how to do a specific task, as a SKILL.md Markdown file in a folder. The YAML front matter must contain a name and a description; the description is the trigger telling the agent when to use it.\nSource: What AI Agent Skills Are and How They Work [01:06] to [03:12]\n\nQ49 [agent-skills | L2] Explain progressive disclosure. Why is it needed?\nAnswer: Loading hundreds of full skills would blow the context window's token budget. So tier one loads only each skill's name and description at startup; tier two loads the full instructions when a request matches a description; tier three loads scripts, references and assets only when the task needs them.\nSource: What AI Agent Skills Are and How They Work [04:12] to [06:18]\n\nQ50 [agent-skills | L3] Compare skills with MCP, RAG and fine-tuning as ways to give an agent knowledge. What are the security risks of skills?\nAnswer: MCP gives tool access, what the agent can reach, but not when or how to use it. RAG gives factual reference material but does not teach how to do something. Fine-tuning bakes knowledge into weights: permanent but expensive, and must be redone when the model changes. Skills give procedural knowledge and are just files: version-controlled, easy to update and portable. Risk: skills can run scripts with access to files, environment variables and API keys, and public skills have been found with prompt injection, tool poisoning and malware, so review them like software dependencies.\nSource: What AI Agent Skills Are and How They Work [06:18] to [08:24], [10:29]\n\nQ51 [memory | L1] What are the four types of agent memory in the CoALA framework?\nAnswer: Working memory (the context window), semantic memory (facts and knowledge, often Markdown files such as CLAUDE.md), procedural memory (how to do things, implemented as skills) and episodic memory (distilled records of past interactions).\nSource: The Four Types of Memory Every AI Agent Needs [01:03] to [08:32]\n\nQ52 [memory | L3] Why is episodic memory the hardest type to get right, and does every agent need all four types?\nAnswer: Saving every transcript is not useful, so systems must distil what is worth remembering, and decide what to delete and when information becomes obsolete (forgetting is an engineering problem). Not every agent needs all four: a reflex agent like a thermostat needs only working memory, a password-reset bot needs working and procedural, a coding agent needs all four.\nSource: The Four Types of Memory Every AI Agent Needs [06:20] to [09:35]\n\nQ53 [reasoning-models | L1] How does a large reasoning model differ from a standard LLM?\nAnswer: It still predicts tokens, but first it \"thinks\": it generates an internal chain of thought to plan, weigh options and check calculations before giving its answer, instead of answering straight away with the statistically likely continuation.\nSource: What Are Large Reasoning Models? [00:00] to [01:06]\n\nQ54 [reasoning-models | L3] When would you NOT use a reasoning model?\nAnswer: When the task is simple, for example a social media post or a factual question, because reasoning costs more compute (more VRAM, energy, a bigger cloud bill) and adds latency. A regular or smaller model is better value there. Use reasoning models for multi-step logic, planning, debugging or complex maths.\nSource: What Are Large Reasoning Models? [01:06], [06:20] to [07:27]; Deep Dive into LLMs (Karpathy) [159:57]\n\nQ55 [ai-stack | L2] Name the five layers of the AI stack and one choice you make at each.\nAnswer: Infrastructure (on premise, cloud or local GPUs); models (open or proprietary, size, specialisation); data (sources, pipelines, vector databases and RAG); orchestration (planning, tool calling, reviewing); application (interface such as text or images with citations, and integrations with other tools).\nSource: What Is an AI Stack? [00:00] to [08:25]\n\nQ56 [prompt-injection | L2] What is the difference between direct and indirect prompt injection?\nAnswer: In direct injection the attacker types the malicious instructions into the system. In indirect injection the instructions are hidden in data the system consumes, such as training data or documents and web pages pulled in by RAG, so an innocent user's request triggers them.\nSource: What Is a Prompt Injection Attack? [04:05] to [05:08]\n\nQ57 [prompt-injection | L3] Why are LLMs fundamentally vulnerable to prompt injection, and what defences would you put in place for an agent that can make payments?\nAnswer: In traditional software, code and user input are separate; in an LLM, instructions and input are both text and the boundary is murky. Defences: least privilege (only the capabilities needed), human in the loop for important actions such as payments, input filtering, curated data, RLHF and tools that scan models and vet API calls. There is no single fix.\nSource: What Is a Prompt Injection Attack? [03:04] to [04:05], [07:09] to [10:14]\n\nQ58 [benchmarks | L2] What are the three steps of running an LLM benchmark, and name two limitations of benchmarks.\nAnswer: Prepare sample data, test the model (zero-shot, few-shot or fine-tuned), and score with metrics such as accuracy, recall or perplexity. Limitations: they may miss edge cases specific to your problem; they can cause overfitting so scores do not reflect unseen data; and they have a finite lifespan once models max them out.\nSource: What are Large Language Model Benchmarks? [00:00] to [02:06], [05:09]\n\nQ59 [evaluating-ai-outputs | L3] What is LLM as a judge, and which biases do you need to guard against?\nAnswer: Using an LLM to evaluate outputs, either by direct assessment against a rubric or by pairwise comparison (A or B). It scales, is flexible and handles subjective qualities without a reference. Biases: positional bias (favours a position), verbosity bias (favours longer answers) and self-enhancement bias (favours its own outputs). Mitigate with checks such as positional swaps.\nSource: LLM as a Judge [00:00] to [05:06]\n\nQ60 [explainability | L2] What is explainable AI, and how could it help a bank whose fraud model flags a customer's transaction?\nAnswer: XAI lets humans understand how a model reaches its results, so they can trust or challenge them. A dashboard could show the main factors (amount, location) and how much each mattered, plus the minimum change that would flip the decision. It also helps detect drift, meet regulatory standards and lets the customer challenge the outcome. Techniques include LIME and DeepLIFT.\nSource: What is Explainable AI? [02:05] to [06:17]\n\nQ61 [synthetic-data | L2] What is synthetic data, and what is its main weakness?\nAnswer: Artificially generated data that replicates the properties of real data, used when real data is scarce or confidential (financial records, medical histories); it is cheap and perfectly labelled. Its main weakness is that it cannot capture the full variety of real-world factors or unanticipated events, like Leicester City winning the league at 5,000 to 1.\nSource: What is Synthetic Data? [01:06] to [04:10]\n\nQ62 [selecting-business-applications | L3] A CFO wants to use generative AI for financial forecasting. What would you advise, using the four considerations from the course?\nAnswer: Forecasting usually does not need GenAI; existing forecasting models do it at a fraction of the cost. Check needs (is GenAI really needed or does existing ML do it at lower TCO), capabilities, integration with the current IT stack, and skills. Match the problem to the technology, and use existing investments where they fit.\nSource: Select the right AI use case for your business [01:05] to [02:08]\n\nQ63 [capabilities-and-limitations | L2] What are the four properties of generative AI in the Anthropic Academy course, and what is meant by \"calibrated trust\"?\nAnswer: Next Token Prediction, Knowledge, Working Memory and Steerability. Each sits on a continuum from capability to limitation, and the same mechanism produces both. Calibrated trust means locating your task on each continuum rather than trusting or distrusting the AI wholesale.\nSource: Anthropic Academy, What We Mean by AI (lesson 2)\n\nQ64 [capabilities-and-limitations | L3] An assistant gives you a summary with a fabricated citation. Using the \"when properties collide\" idea, which properties are involved and what is the fix?\nAnswer: Next Token Prediction meets Knowledge: the model generates plausible specifics (fabrication concentrates in names, dates, citations) about something poorly represented in its training data. The fix is to verify specifics and supply the source or use retrieval or search.\nSource: Anthropic Academy, When Properties Collide (lesson 12) and Next Token Prediction (lesson 4)\n</question_bank>";
